   "texts": {
    "profile": "TITLE: Photosynthesis: Building Sugar From Light\n\nINTRO: Nothing here is scored, so a wrong answer only shows what to look at next. Photosynthesis is how a plant makes its own food: it builds glucose, a sugar, from carbon dioxide and water, powered by light.\n\nSECTION 1 - The reaction in one line\nRule: a plant makes glucose from carbon dioxide and water, and light energy from the sun powers the reaction. Read it as an arrow chain: carbon dioxide + water, plus light energy, gives glucose + oxygen. The carbon dioxide comes in through the leaves and the water comes up from the roots. Without light the chain stops, no matter how much carbon dioxide and water are present.\nEXAMPLE: Try it: A plant in a sealed jar has plenty of carbon dioxide and water but sits in darkness. Which input is missing? Answer: light energy — the other two inputs are already there.\n\nSECTION 2 - Where the light gets caught\nChlorophyll is the green pigment that absorbs the light energy. It sits inside chloroplasts, and chloroplasts are packed into leaf cells, so photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves. Any part without chlorophyll, such as a root, cannot run the reaction.\nEXAMPLE: Try it: A comic panel shows a leaf with white patches labelled 'no chloroplasts'. Should that patch be drawn giving off oxygen in bright light? Answer: no — no chloroplasts means no chlorophyll, so no light is absorbed.\n\nSECTION 3 - Oxygen leaves as a by-product\nOxygen is a by-product: the plant does not use it in this reaction, it releases it. That is why a water plant in bright light streams out bubbles. The oxygen comes from the water molecules the plant splits, not from the carbon dioxide.\nEXAMPLE: Try it: In a video game, a plant sprite releases bubbles only while a light beam is on it. What gas is in the bubbles? Answer: oxygen.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Using 6 carbon dioxide molecules and 6 water molecules to make 1 glucose molecule (C6H12O6) and 6 oxygen molecules (O2), check that every type of atom balances.\n  1. Count carbon on the left: 6 CO2 holds 6 carbon atoms. On the right, glucose C6H12O6 holds 6. Carbon balances, 6 and 6.\n  2. Count hydrogen on the left: 6 H2O holds 6 x 2 = 12 hydrogen atoms. On the right, glucose holds 12. Hydrogen balances, 12 and 12.\n  3. Count oxygen on the left: 6 CO2 holds 6 x 2 = 12 oxygen atoms, and 6 H2O adds 6 more, so 12 + 6 = 18.\n  4. Count oxygen on the right: glucose holds 6, and 6 O2 holds 6 x 2 = 12, so 6 + 12 = 18. Oxygen balances, 18 and 18.\n  ANSWER: 6CO2 + 6H2O -> C6H12O6 + 6O2. Carbon 6 and 6, hydrogen 12 and 12, oxygen 18 and 18: everything balances.\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\nWrong: carbon dioxide + water + oxygen -> glucose. Why: oxygen has been put on the input side, but the plant releases oxygen rather than taking it in, so the equation now describes a plant that consumes the gas it actually gives off. Right: carbon dioxide + water, plus light energy, -> glucose + oxygen.\n\nSUMMARY: A plant uses light energy, absorbed by chlorophyll in the chloroplasts of its leaves, to turn carbon dioxide and water into glucose, and it releases oxygen as a by-product.\n\nCHECK QUESTIONS:\n1. A leaf is photosynthesising in bright sunlight. Which substance does it release? [choices: carbon dioxide | water | oxygen | glucose] -> oxygen\n2. A plant is well supplied with water and carbon dioxide but kept in a dark cupboard. Explain why it stops making glucose. Type your answer. [typed answer] -> There is no light, so chlorophyll in the chloroplasts has no light energy to absorb, and the reaction cannot run.",
    "mirror": "TITLE: Photosynthesis: How Plants Make Sugar\n\nINTRO: Photosynthesis is the way plants make their own food. The food is a sugar called glucose. Plants do this mainly in their leaves. You will see the starting materials, the energy source, and what comes out.\n\nSECTION 1 - The recipe for glucose\nPhotosynthesis starts with two materials: carbon dioxide and water. Carbon dioxide is a gas in the air. Water is taken in by the roots. Light energy from the sun supplies the power. The plant turns these into glucose, and oxygen is released as a by-product, which is something extra made along the way.\nEXAMPLE: Think of a video game crafting recipe. Two materials plus a power source make a health item plus a spare spark. In a leaf, the two materials are carbon dioxide and water, the power source is light, the health item is glucose, and the spare spark is oxygen.\n\nSECTION 2 - Chlorophyll catches the light\nLeaves contain tiny green parts inside their cells called chloroplasts. Inside chloroplasts is a green pigment called chlorophyll. A pigment is a material that gives colour. Chlorophyll absorbs light energy. That light energy powers the making of glucose.\nEXAMPLE: If you draw a comic panel of a leaf, draw tiny green oval machines inside the leaf cells. Label these chloroplasts. Inside each machine, draw green paint called chlorophyll. The green paint catches the sunlight.\n\nSECTION 3 - Why leaves are the main place\nPhotosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves. Leaves are flat and wide, so they catch a lot of light. They also have many chloroplasts. Roots take in water, but they have no chlorophyll and get little light. So roots are not the main place for photosynthesis.\nEXAMPLE: A basketball team scores mainly near the hoop, not in the locker room. A plant makes glucose mainly in its leaves, not in its roots. Try it: A leaf takes in carbon dioxide and water and gets sunlight. Fill the blank: carbon dioxide + water -> ____ + oxygen. Answer: glucose.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A bean leaf has water from its roots and carbon dioxide from the air. Sunlight shines on the leaf. Write the word equation for photosynthesis and say where the light is absorbed.\n  1. List the starting materials. The leaf takes in carbon dioxide from the air and water from the roots.\n  2. Name the energy source. The light energy comes from the sun.\n  3. Say where the light is caught. Chlorophyll in the chloroplasts of the leaf absorbs the light energy.\n  4. Name what is made. The plant makes glucose, a sugar.\n  5. Name the by-product. Oxygen is released as a by-product.\n  6. Put the parts in a word equation. A word equation is a short way to show starting materials and what is made: carbon dioxide + water -> glucose + oxygen. Light energy and chlorophyll make this happen.\n  ANSWER: Word equation: carbon dioxide + water -> glucose + oxygen. The light is absorbed by chlorophyll in the chloroplasts.\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\nWrong: Plants use oxygen and water to make glucose. Why: oxygen is not a starting material for photosynthesis; it is the by-product released. The carbon building block comes from carbon dioxide. Right: carbon dioxide + water -> glucose + oxygen.\nWrong: Chlorophyll makes glucose in the leaf. Why: chlorophyll does not build glucose; it absorbs light energy. The glucose building happens in the chloroplasts using that light. Right: chlorophyll in the chloroplasts absorbs light energy, which powers the making of glucose.\nWrong: Photosynthesis happens mainly in the roots because roots take in water. Why: roots do take in water, but they do not have chlorophyll and get little light, so they are not the main site. Right: photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves, where chlorophyll catches light.\n\nSUMMARY: Plants make glucose from carbon dioxide and water. They use light energy from the sun. Chlorophyll in chloroplasts absorbs the light. Oxygen is released as a by-product. Photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves.\n\nCHECK QUESTIONS:\n1. Which gas do plants take in to make glucose during photosynthesis? Hint: it is the gas you breathe out. [choices: Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Nitrogen | Hydrogen] -> Carbon dioxide\n2. Where does chlorophyll absorb light energy? Hint: look for the tiny green parts inside leaf cells. [choices: In the roots | In the chloroplasts | In the stem | In the flower] -> In the chloroplasts\n3. A plant has water and carbon dioxide, and light shines on its leaves. Name the sugar it makes and the gas it releases. Hint: one is a sugar, one is a gas you breathe in. [typed answer] -> glucose and oxygen"
   }
